# Migration: EventProcessor to .NET 8

## Key Problems in .NET 8

| .NET Framework API | .NET 8 Status |
|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | **Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** (removed in .NET 5+) |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Still works but unreliable — only interrupts in `WaitSleepJoin` state |
| `ManualResetEvent` + manual `Queue` | Works but error-prone (race conditions on multiple items) |

## Race Condition Bug in Original Code

If `Enqueue` is called twice before `RunLoop` wakes, `_newEvent.Set()` is called twice but the event is already set — **second signal is lost**. Only one item gets dequeued per wake cycle.

---

## Migrated Code (Modern .NET 8)

```csharp
using System.Threading.Channels;

public class EventProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private readonly Channel<string> _channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<string>();
    private Task? _processingTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _processingTask = Task.Run(() => RunLoopAsync(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task RunLoopAsync(CancellationToken ct)
    {
        await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(ct))
        {
            Handle(item);
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        if (!_channel.Writer.TryWrite(item))
            throw new InvalidOperationException("Channel is closed.");
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        _channel.Writer.Complete();           // signals no more items
        _cts?.Cancel();                       // cancels if blocked waiting
        if (_processingTask is not null)
            await _processingTask.ConfigureAwait(false);
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item)
    {
        // your processing logic
    }
}
```

---

## Why This Is Better

| Concern | Old | New |
|---|---|---|
| Shutdown | `Thread.Abort` (broken in .NET 8) | `CancellationToken` + channel completion |
| Lost signals | Race when multiple enqueues | Channel handles backpressure correctly |
| Thread management | Manual `Thread` | `Task.Run` with structured cleanup |
| Blocking | `ManualResetEvent` + lock | Lock-free `Channel<T>` with async enumeration |

---

## Pitfalls to Watch For

1. **`Thread.Abort` → `PlatformNotSupportedException`** — This is the hard blocker. Always replace with cooperative cancellation (`CancellationToken`).

2. **`Thread.Interrupt` is unreliable** — It only works if the thread is in a `WaitSleepJoin` state at that exact moment. Use `CancellationToken` instead.

3. **Don't swallow `OperationCanceledException`** — If `Handle()` is async, cancelled tasks throw this. Let it propagate to exit the loop cleanly.

4. **Graceful vs. immediate shutdown** — The code above drains remaining items. If you want to stop immediately (drop pending items), just cancel without calling `_channel.Writer.Complete()`:

```csharp
public async ValueTask StopImmediatelyAsync()
{
    _cts?.Cancel();
    if (_processingTask is not null)
    {
        try { await _processingTask.ConfigureAwait(false); }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) { }
    }
}
```

5. **Bounded channels for backpressure** — If producers can outpace consumers, use `Channel.CreateBounded<string>(capacity)` to apply backpressure.

6. **Exception handling** — If `Handle` can throw, wrap it so one bad item doesn't kill the loop:

```csharp
private async Task RunLoopAsync(CancellationToken ct)
{
    await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(ct))
    {
        try { Handle(item); }
        catch (Exception ex) when (ex is not OperationCanceledException)
        {
            // log and continue
        }
    }
}
```

---

**TL;DR**: Replace `Thread` + `ManualResetEvent` + `Thread.Abort` with `Channel<T>` + `Task.Run` + `CancellationToken`. This is the idiomatic .NET 8 pattern — thread-safe, allocation-efficient, and cooperatively cancellable.
